/-!
# Noetherian directed colimits of regular local rings

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 07DX, `lemma-colimit-regular`
(blueprint: lem-per-noetherian-local-colimit-regular).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory CategoryTheory.Limits

namespace IsRegularLocalRing

/-- A Noetherian colimit of a nonempty directed system of regular local rings with local
transition maps is regular local. The functor laws express the identity and composition
laws of the transition homomorphisms. No injectivity or flatness is assumed.

Source: Stacks Project, Algebra, Tag 07DX, `lemma-colimit-regular`
(blueprint: lem-per-noetherian-local-colimit-regular).

Proof:
1. By `lem-pc-filtered-colimit-commrings-elementwise`, every element has a representative
   at a stage and equality holds at a common later stage. The images of the maximal ideals
   form an ideal m: move representatives to a common stage to check addition and ideal
   multiplication. It is proper, since an element of a stage's maximal ideal cannot become
   one at a later stage under local transition maps.
2. If an element is outside m, any representative is outside its stage's maximal ideal,
   hence is a unit by `lem-weil-pbc-local-ring-unit-criterion`. Thus the colimit is local
   with maximal ideal m. Each coprojection has inverse image of m equal to the maximal
   ideal at its stage: an element outside that ideal is a unit and stays a unit.
3. Each stage is a domain by `lem-sc-regular-local-domain`. A zero product in the colimit
   is represented at a common stage and becomes zero at a later stage. One factor there
   vanishes, so one factor in the colimit vanishes. Together with properness of m this
   proves the colimit is a domain, without injectivity of the transition maps.
4. For all such systems, induct strongly on the least number n of generators of m,
   finite by Noetherianity. If n = 0 the colimit is a field and is regular. Otherwise choose
   minimal generators x, x₂, ..., xₙ. Then x is outside m²: otherwise Nakayama, applied to
   the finite module m (`lem-weil-pbc-nakayama-local-generators`), removes x from this
   generating set, contradicting minimality. In particular x is nonzero.
5. Lift x to xᵢ in a stage's maximal ideal. At every later stage j its image xⱼ is in
   the maximal ideal but outside its square, since membership in that square would put x
   in m². The quotients Lⱼ/(xⱼ) are regular local by
   `lem-weil-pbc-regular-local-hypersurface-criterion`; the transition maps descend.
6. The tail above i is nonempty and directed, with the same colimit: extend a compatible
   cocone by choosing common upper bounds, with independence checked at another upper
   bound. The quotient colimit is L/(x), since compatible ring maps from the stage
   quotients correspond exactly to compatible maps killing xⱼ, hence to maps from L
   killing x. This verifies the universal property `def-weil-pbc-category-colimit`.
7. The quotient stages and L/(x) have maximal ideals the images of their original maximal
   ideals. The quotient transition maps are local by the inverse-image criterion. The
   colimit quotient is Noetherian, and its maximal ideal is generated by the images of
   x₂, ..., xₙ. Its least generator number is at most n - 1, so induction makes it regular.
8. By step 3 the nonzero x is a nonzerodivisor. The theorem
   `lem-weil-pbc-dimension-quotient-nonzerodivisor` gives dim L = dim L/(x) + 1.
   Lift dim L/(x) generators of the quotient maximal ideal to m and adjoin x. They
   generate m and number dim L, proving regularity and completing the induction.
-/
theorem of_noetherian_colimit
    {I : Type u} [PartialOrder I] [Nonempty I] [IsDirected I (· ≤ ·)]
    (F : I ⥤ CommRingCat.{u}) [HasColimit F]
    [∀ i : I, IsRegularLocalRing (F.obj i)]
    (hlocal : ∀ (i j : I) (hij : i ≤ j), IsLocalHom (F.map (homOfLE hij)).hom)
    [IsNoetherianRing (colimit F : CommRingCat.{u})] :
    IsRegularLocalRing (colimit F : CommRingCat.{u}) := by
  sorry

end IsRegularLocalRing
